<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pausanias (general)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 470 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Pausanias the regent · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agiad-dynasty">Agiad dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pausanias was a Spartan general and regent for the young king Pleistarchus. He commanded the Greek army that defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> in 479 BCE and in 478 BCE took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. His arrogance towards the allies led them to turn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, which formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a>. Accused of intrigue with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, he took refuge in a temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and was starved to death there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pausanias-general.jpg" alt="Pausanias (general)" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jona Lendering · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pausanias_in_the_Capitoline_Museums,_Rome.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pausanias belonged to the Agiad royal house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. He was a son of the regent Cleombrotus and a nephew of the recently dead king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i">Leonidas I</a>, but not in the direct line of succession. After Leonidas's death, while the king's son Pleistarchus was still a minor, Pausanias served as regent. He was the father of Pleistoanax, who later became king. What is known of his life comes largely from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>, Diodorus and a few other classical sources.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 479 BCE Pausanias led the Spartan army alongside Euryanax, since Pleistarchus was too young to command, and headed the combined land forces of the Hellenic League. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> faced the Persian commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a> along the Asopos River. After eleven days of stalemate the Persian cavalry fouled the river from which the Greeks drew water, and the Athenians moved in the night. Mardonius took the move for flight and sent his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> against Pausanias, who led the Greeks to a major victory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> judged it the most glorious victory known to him.</p><p>In 478 BCE, after the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, he was accused of releasing prisoners who were relatives of the Persian king, and of writing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> offering to bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> under Persian control in return for marriage to the king's daughter. He was said to have adopted Persian dress and customs. For want of evidence he was acquitted. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> the Athenians and many allies resented his arrogance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Spartans recalled him again in 477 BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a> imprisoned him but released him for lack of evidence, although some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a> reported that he had offered them freedom if they joined a revolt. Later one of his messengers produced a written letter. Pursued by the ephors, Pausanias took refuge in the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> of the Brazen House. His mother Theano is said to have laid a brick at the door, and the Spartans blocked the doorway and he starved to death. The Oracle of Delphi showed displeasure at the violation of a suppliant's sanctity, and the Spartans set up two bronze statues of him at the temple.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pausanias the Regent” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 478 BCE the Greek fleet under the Spartan regent <b>Pausanias</b> captured Byzantium from the Persians; his arrogance there helped drive the allies to accept Athenian leadership.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The behaviour of the Spartan general <b>Pausanias</b> alienated many of the Allies and led to his recall.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delian-league">Delian League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 480 BCE Leonidas I held the pass of Thermopylae with a small Greek force including 300 Spartiates until he was outflanked and killed, and in 479 BCE the regent <b>Pausanias</b> commanded the Greek army that destroyed the Persian land forces at the Battle of Plataea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Aristotle and others called Tartessos a river, and <b>Pausanias</b> described a river with two mouths and a city of the same name between them, which the later Baetis, now the Guadalquivir, seems to fit.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tartessos">Tartessos · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The arrogance of the Spartan regent <b>Pausanias</b> at Byzantium in 478 BCE led the Ionian allies to turn to Athens, which founded the Delian League.</blockquote><a href="/state/hellenic-alliance">Hellenic Alliance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 479 BCE the allied Greek army under the Spartan <b>Pausanias</b> defeated the Persian general Mardonius on its territory, and the city was charged with tending the graves of the fallen and hosting a festival of freedom, the Eleutheria.</blockquote><a href="/state/plataea">Plataea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.07,22.43&amp;year=-480" aria-label="Sparta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5112 828 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pausanias (general)"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5562 1098h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5562 1098h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.07,22.43&amp;year=-480">What was here in 480 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
